{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested at exact clean df7095298c48b248d2ca77826715a7865ccd7cb8 (one commit over main e0c83a8, 45 exact-path artifacts, primary notes/2026-08-28-tailapp-architecture.md), on one narrow point; everything else is sound and the correction stays on this commitment with fresh exact-head review. Blocking finding: the host projection's empty-MANY line, internal/projection/projection.go:565 (values := make([]map[string]any, 0)), is load-bearing and has no host-level proof. Reverting it to a nil slice leaves internal/projection, internal/engine and tailapps green (ok 0.44 s / 1.25 s / 1.28 s), while the identical revert in the corpus harness copy at jsonataddl/corpus_projection_test.go:193 is caught ('input rows read \"marks\" must be an array of at most 10 rows'). A nil slice marshals to null, validateInputRows' Many case refuses it, and in the host that refusal is a Process error that records a gap: a regression would permanently gap every projection on any delivery where a MANY read returns zero rows, which is exactly the semantic this head changes (the settle-first marks_seen 1 to 0 outcome). No host fixture exercises a MANY read at all (grep finds only the compiler refusal case). Add one host-level fixture with a MANY read returning zero rows whose test goes red under that revert; no other change requested. Verified at this head in a fresh detached checkout: go vet clean; go test ./... and go test -race ./... ok (15 packages, 63 s / 58 s); jsonataddl with GOWORK=off vet, test and race ok. Seven omission mutants reproduced red and restored: byte and depth bounds before the contract walk (input.go:28-33; over-depth and over-byte inputs accepted), engine runtime guard before journal detach (engine.go:626-632; continue changed obligations to nil, losing queued work, which ContinueCompatible cannot catch because recovery compiles old source with the current runtime), contract admission collision and scalar type switch (input_contract.go:94,